//! crypto/bip32.rye — BIP-32 hierarchical-deterministic keys, authored in pure Rye as a COMPOSITION over stones already GREEN in this tree,
//! authoring NO new cryptography. A single seed becomes a whole tree of keys: from one master, a keeper derives a child by an index, that child's
//! child, and so on down a path — every node reproducible from the seed, and any node exportable as an extended public key that derives the same
//! public tree without ever touching the private half. This is the rung a wallet's whole account model stands on: one backup phrase, one seed, and
//! the entire ladder of addresses beneath it.
//!
//! Season: crypto (Season G · Cryptography), a composition on the shelf beside eth_address, bitcoin_address, and the EIP transaction digests. It
//! contributes only the BIP-32 orchestration — the child-key-derivation function, the extended-key serialization envelope, and the parent
//! fingerprint — over rungs each proven byte-for-byte on metal: HMAC-SHA-512 (crypto/hmac_sha512.rye) is the derivation PRF, secp256k1 public-key
//! derivation and SEC1 compression (crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye) turn a private scalar into serP(point(k)), the scalar field's reduction modulo the
//! group order n (crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye) finishes the child key (IL + k_par) mod n, HASH160 (crypto/bitcoin_address.rye) gives the four-byte
//! parent fingerprint, and Base58Check (encoding/base58check.rye) wraps the 78-byte extended key into its "xprv…" / "xpub…" text.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G · the priority spine: Rye first).
//! Clean-room: BIP-32 is a public standard (Bitcoin BIP-32); the assembly is ours, standing on our own parity-GREEN rungs, never a copied line
//! (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX — a child private key is k_child = (parse256(I_L) + k_par) mod n, where I = HMAC-SHA-512(key = c_par, data = D) splits into a left half
//! I_L that offsets the parent scalar and a right half I_R that becomes the child chain code. The data D is what distinguishes the two derivation
//! kinds: for a HARDENED child (index ≥ 2³¹) D = 0x00 ‖ ser256(k_par) ‖ ser32(i), feeding the private key itself so no public-only party can derive
//! it; for a NORMAL child D = serP(point(k_par)) ‖ ser32(i), feeding the compressed public key so the same offset can be reached from an extended
//! PUBLIC key alone. The master key is the same HMAC over the fixed key "Bitcoin seed" and the raw seed. This module derives the master, both child
//! kinds, and serializes any node as xprv or xpub — proving the whole ladder against BIP-32's own published Test Vector 1.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. Every scalar handled here is a TEST key derived from BIP-32's
//! published seed 000102…0f, never the maintainer's own identity key (that stays the custody gate #3/#4). Deriving and serializing keys touches no
//! network and moves no value. Parity is proven the replayable way the tree favours — against BIP-32's own published Test Vector 1: from the
//! published seed, every one of the six chain nodes (m, m/0ₕ, m/0ₕ/1, m/0ₕ/1/2ₕ, m/0ₕ/1/2ₕ/2, m/0ₕ/1/2ₕ/2/1000000000) serializes to EXACTLY the
//! spec's published xprv AND xpub base58 strings, character for character — so a one-bit error anywhere in the HMAC, the modular add, the
//! compression, the fingerprint, or the envelope would break the match. No fabricated constant: the answers are the spec's own.
//!
//! The public tree, too: neuter turns an extended private key into an extended PUBLIC key (its private scalar replaced by serP(point(k))), and
//! ckd_pub derives a normal child directly from a public parent — K_child = point(parse256(I_L)) + K_par over the complete secp256k1 group law —
//! so a watch-only wallet carries an xpub and derives the same public addresses without ever holding a private key. A hardened index is refused
//! from a public parent (hardening needs the private key it does not hold). Proven both ways against Test Vector 1: every node's neuter reproduces
//! the spec's xpub, and for each normal index CKDpub reaches that node's xpub from the neutered parent alone.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/bip32.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const hmac = @import("hmac_sha512.rye");
const pk = @import("secp256k1_pubkey.rye");
const sc = @import("secp256k1_scalar.rye");
const pt = @import("secp256k1_group.rye");
const fe = @import("fe_secp256k1.rye");
const bc = @import("bitcoin_address.rye");
const sha256 = @import("sha256.rye");
const ripemd = @import("ripemd160.rye");
const b58c = @import("encoding_base58check.rye");

pub const bip32_version = "20260816.140000";

/// A private scalar, a chain code, and a coordinate are each 32 bytes big-endian — the width secp256k1 and SHA-512's right half fix.
pub const key_bytes: u32 = 32;
/// The HMAC-SHA-512 output is 64 bytes: the left 32 (I_L) offset the parent scalar, the right 32 (I_R) become the child chain code.
pub const i_bytes: u32 = 64;
/// A serialized public key or a hardened-derivation key body is 33 bytes: a leading byte (SEC1 tag, or the 0x00 that pads a private key to a
/// public key's width) then the 32-byte coordinate/scalar.
pub const key_data_bytes: u32 = 1 + key_bytes; // 33
/// A child index is a 32-bit big-endian number; indices at or above this offset are HARDENED (they feed the private key into the HMAC).
pub const hardened_offset: u32 = 0x80000000;
/// The parent fingerprint is the first four bytes of HASH160(serP(point(k))) — enough to identify a parent, never enough to reconstruct it.
pub const fingerprint_bytes: u32 = 4;
/// The CKD data block is serP(point(k_par)) or 0x00‖ser256(k_par), then ser32(index): 33 + 4 = 37 bytes.
pub const ckd_data_bytes: u32 = key_data_bytes + 4; // 37
/// The extended-key serialization is 78 bytes: version(4) ‖ depth(1) ‖ parent_fingerprint(4) ‖ child_number(4) ‖ chain_code(32) ‖ key_data(33).
pub const ext_serialized_bytes: u32 = 4 + 1 + 4 + 4 + key_bytes + key_data_bytes; // 78
/// A seed is between 128 and 512 bits (BIP-32 §Master key generation) — 16 to 64 bytes.
pub const min_seed_bytes: u32 = 16;
pub const max_seed_bytes: u32 = 64;

/// The mainnet version bytes BIP-32 fixes: xprv = 0x0488ADE4, xpub = 0x0488B21E. Named rather than scattered as magic four-byte prefixes.
pub const version_xprv: [4]u8 = .{ 0x04, 0x88, 0xAD, 0xE4 };
pub const version_xpub: [4]u8 = .{ 0x04, 0x88, 0xB2, 0x1E };

/// The fixed HMAC key BIP-32 seeds the master from — the ASCII string "Bitcoin seed".
const master_hmac_key = "Bitcoin seed";

/// The faults this module raises, each refused at the edge before a partial or dishonest result escapes. Composed with the errors of the stones
/// underneath so a caller handles one set.
pub const Bip32Error = error{
    /// A seed outside the 16–64 byte range BIP-32 permits.
    BadSeedLength,
    /// A master derivation whose left half I_L is not a canonical scalar (≥ n) or is zero — BIP-32 declares such a seed invalid.
    InvalidSeed,
    /// A child derivation whose I_L is ≥ n, or whose resulting scalar is zero — BIP-32 declares such an index invalid (a caller proceeds to i+1).
    InvalidChild,
    /// A public parent asked to derive a HARDENED child (index ≥ 2³¹) — impossible, because hardening feeds the private key the public parent
    /// does not hold (BIP-32 §CKDpub).
    HardenedFromPublic,
    /// A serialization destination buffer too small for the Base58Check text.
    DestTooSmall,
} || hmac.HmacError || pk.PubkeyError || b58c.Base58CheckError || sha256.Sha256Error || ripemd.Ripemd160Error;

/// An extended key node: the private scalar plus the four fields that place it in the tree. Depth 0, an all-zero fingerprint, and an all-zero
/// child number mark the master. The private key is always 32 bytes big-endian; the public half is derived on demand for xpub serialization.
pub const ExtKey = struct {
    depth: u8,
    parent_fp: [fingerprint_bytes]u8,
    child_num: [4]u8, // big-endian
    chain_code: [key_bytes]u8,
    key: [key_bytes]u8, // private scalar, big-endian
};

/// Reverse a 32-byte encoding between big-endian (BIP-32's convention for scalars and coordinates) and little-endian (the order the scalar field
/// walks). The boundary courtesy so the arithmetic stays in its native order.
fn reverse32(s: *const [key_bytes]u8) [key_bytes]u8 {
    var r: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < key_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        // invariant: source and mirror index both stay inside the 32-byte width.
        assert(i < key_bytes and (key_bytes - 1 - i) < key_bytes);
        r[i] = s[key_bytes - 1 - i];
    }
    return r;
}

/// True when a 32-byte big-endian value is all zero — the "scalar is zero" gate BIP-32 refuses for both master and child.
fn is_zero32(s: *const [key_bytes]u8) bool {
    var acc: u8 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < key_bytes) : (i += 1) acc |= s[i];
    return acc == 0;
}

/// Serialize a 32-bit index big-endian — BIP-32's ser32, the four bytes the derivation data and the extended key both carry.
fn ser32(index: u32) [4]u8 {
    return .{
        @truncate(index >> 24),
        @truncate(index >> 16),
        @truncate(index >> 8),
        @truncate(index),
    };
}

/// Add two 32-byte big-endian scalars modulo n: (a + b) mod n. The 257-bit sum lands in the low bytes of a 64-byte little-endian wide buffer — the
/// exact shape the GREEN crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye reduce folds — and reduce returns the canonical residue, which we mirror back to big-endian.
/// This authors NO new primitive; it lays the sum out for the proven reduction. Both inputs are below n, so the sum is below 2n < 2²⁵⁷.
fn add_mod_n(a_be: *const [key_bytes]u8, b_be: *const [key_bytes]u8) [key_bytes]u8 {
    var wide = [_]u8{0} ** 64;
    var carry: u16 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < key_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        // invariant: little-endian byte i comes from big-endian byte 31−i of each input; the column stays inside both widths.
        assert((key_bytes - 1 - i) < key_bytes and i < 64);
        const s: u16 = @as(u16, a_be[key_bytes - 1 - i]) + @as(u16, b_be[key_bytes - 1 - i]) + carry;
        wide[i] = @truncate(s);
        carry = s >> 8;
    }
    // invariant: the final carry is a single bit — a + b < 2n < 2²⁵⁷ occupies at most the 33rd byte.
    assert(carry <= 1);
    wide[key_bytes] = @truncate(carry);
    const reduced_le = sc.reduce(&wide);
    return reverse32(&reduced_le);
}

/// The compressed public key serP(point(k)) of a private scalar — 33 bytes, the GREEN crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye derivation. The private scalar
/// computes only the PUBLIC point; the result carries no secret.
fn serialize_public(key: *const [key_bytes]u8) Bip32Error![key_data_bytes]u8 {
    return pk.from_private_compressed(key);
}

/// The four-byte parent fingerprint of an extended key: the first four bytes of HASH160(serP(point(k))). Composes the GREEN HASH160 over the
/// compressed public key — enough to identify a parent in a serialized child, never enough to reconstruct it.
pub fn fingerprint(ext: *const ExtKey) Bip32Error![fingerprint_bytes]u8 {
    const pub_key = try serialize_public(&ext.key);
    const h = try bc.hash160(&pub_key);
    // invariant: HASH160 is twenty bytes; the fingerprint takes exactly the leading four.
    comptime assert(bc.hash160_bytes >= fingerprint_bytes);
    var out: [fingerprint_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < fingerprint_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        assert(i < fingerprint_bytes);
        out[i] = h[i];
    }
    return out;
}

/// Derive the master extended key from a seed: I = HMAC-SHA-512("Bitcoin seed", seed); I_L is the master private scalar, I_R the master chain code.
/// A seed whose I_L is not a canonical nonzero scalar is refused (BIP-32 §Master key generation).
pub fn master_from_seed(seed: []const u8) Bip32Error!ExtKey {
    // invariant: a seed is 16 to 64 bytes — refused before the HMAC runs.
    if (seed.len < min_seed_bytes or seed.len > max_seed_bytes) return error.BadSeedLength;

    const i = try hmac.mac(seed, master_hmac_key);
    // invariant: HMAC-SHA-512 gives exactly 64 bytes — the two 32-byte halves this derivation splits.
    comptime assert(hmac.tag_len == i_bytes);

    var il: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var ir: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < key_bytes) : (k += 1) {
        assert(k < key_bytes and (key_bytes + k) < i_bytes);
        il[k] = i[k];
        ir[k] = i[key_bytes + k];
    }

    // invariant: the master scalar I_L is canonical (< n) and nonzero — a seed failing this is invalid, not silently reduced.
    const il_le = reverse32(&il);
    if (!sc.is_canonical(&il_le) or is_zero32(&il)) return error.InvalidSeed;

    return ExtKey{
        .depth = 0,
        .parent_fp = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0 },
        .child_num = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0 },
        .chain_code = ir,
        .key = il,
    };
}

/// Derive a child extended key (CKDpriv). For a hardened index the HMAC data is 0x00 ‖ ser256(k_par) ‖ ser32(i); for a normal index it is
/// serP(point(k_par)) ‖ ser32(i). The child scalar is (parse256(I_L) + k_par) mod n and the child chain code is I_R. An I_L ≥ n or a zero child
/// scalar is refused (BIP-32 §CKDpriv) — a caller then tries the next index.
pub fn ckd_priv(parent: *const ExtKey, index: u32) Bip32Error!ExtKey {
    // invariant: depth never wraps — BIP-32 bounds a path to 255 levels, far above any real derivation.
    if (parent.depth == 255) return error.InvalidChild;

    var data: [ckd_data_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    if (index >= hardened_offset) {
        // Hardened: feed the private key, padded to a public key's width by a leading 0x00.
        data[0] = 0x00;
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < key_bytes) : (j += 1) {
            assert((1 + j) < key_data_bytes);
            data[1 + j] = parent.key[j];
        }
    } else {
        // Normal: feed the compressed public key, so this same offset is reachable from an extended public key alone.
        const pub_key = try serialize_public(&parent.key);
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < key_data_bytes) : (j += 1) {
            assert(j < key_data_bytes);
            data[j] = pub_key[j];
        }
    }
    const idx = ser32(index);
    var j: u32 = 0;
    while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
        assert((key_data_bytes + j) < ckd_data_bytes);
        data[key_data_bytes + j] = idx[j];
    }

    const i = try hmac.mac(&data, &parent.chain_code);
    var il: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var ir: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < key_bytes) : (k += 1) {
        assert(k < key_bytes and (key_bytes + k) < i_bytes);
        il[k] = i[k];
        ir[k] = i[key_bytes + k];
    }

    // invariant: I_L must be a canonical scalar (< n); BIP-32 refuses I_L ≥ n rather than reducing it.
    const il_le = reverse32(&il);
    if (!sc.is_canonical(&il_le)) return error.InvalidChild;

    const child_key = add_mod_n(&il, &parent.key);
    // invariant: the child scalar is nonzero — a zero child key is refused, not emitted.
    if (is_zero32(&child_key)) return error.InvalidChild;

    const fp = try fingerprint(parent);
    return ExtKey{
        .depth = parent.depth + 1,
        .parent_fp = fp,
        .child_num = idx,
        .chain_code = ir,
        .key = child_key,
    };
}

/// Lay out the 78-byte extended-key body under `version`, with `key_data` the 33-byte payload (0x00‖ser256(k) for xprv, serP(point(k)) for xpub).
fn frame_bytes(ext: *const ExtKey, version: *const [4]u8, key_data: *const [key_data_bytes]u8) [ext_serialized_bytes]u8 {
    var frame: [ext_serialized_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var off: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
        assert(off + i < ext_serialized_bytes);
        frame[off + i] = version[i];
    }
    off += 4;
    frame[off] = ext.depth;
    off += 1;
    i = 0;
    while (i < fingerprint_bytes) : (i += 1) frame[off + i] = ext.parent_fp[i];
    off += fingerprint_bytes;
    i = 0;
    while (i < 4) : (i += 1) frame[off + i] = ext.child_num[i];
    off += 4;
    i = 0;
    while (i < key_bytes) : (i += 1) frame[off + i] = ext.chain_code[i];
    off += key_bytes;
    i = 0;
    while (i < key_data_bytes) : (i += 1) frame[off + i] = key_data[i];
    off += key_data_bytes;
    // invariant: the frame is filled to exactly its declared width — version, fields, and key data with nothing left.
    assert(off == ext_serialized_bytes);
    return frame;
}

/// Wrap a filled 78-byte frame in Base58Check into `dest`, returning the "xprv…"/"xpub…" text. The 78-byte frame already carries its 4-byte
/// version, so the Base58Check envelope takes the first byte as its version and the remaining 77 as payload — the checksum runs over the whole 78,
/// exactly BIP-32's serialization.
fn base58_frame(dest: []u8, frame: *const [ext_serialized_bytes]u8) Bip32Error![]const u8 {
    // invariant: the frame's leading version byte and its 77-byte tail together are the Base58Check body.
    comptime assert(ext_serialized_bytes == 78);
    return b58c.encode(dest, frame[0], frame[1..ext_serialized_bytes]) catch |err| switch (err) {
        error.DestTooSmall => error.DestTooSmall,
        else => err,
    };
}

/// Serialize an extended key as its "xprv…" text: the private form, key data 0x00 ‖ ser256(k).
pub fn serialize_xprv(dest: []u8, ext: *const ExtKey) Bip32Error![]const u8 {
    var key_data: [key_data_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    key_data[0] = 0x00;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < key_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        assert((1 + i) < key_data_bytes);
        key_data[1 + i] = ext.key[i];
    }
    const frame = frame_bytes(ext, &version_xprv, &key_data);
    return base58_frame(dest, &frame);
}

/// Serialize an extended key as its "xpub…" text: the neutered public form, key data serP(point(k)). This is the neutering BIP-32 names — the
/// public tree without the private half.
pub fn serialize_xpub(dest: []u8, ext: *const ExtKey) Bip32Error![]const u8 {
    const key_data = try serialize_public(&ext.key);
    const frame = frame_bytes(ext, &version_xpub, &key_data);
    return base58_frame(dest, &frame);
}

/// An extended PUBLIC key node: the neutered form of an ExtKey. It carries the compressed public key serP(K) instead of the private scalar, and
/// derives the same public tree over NORMAL indices without ever holding a private key — the whole point of an "xpub" a watch-only wallet imports.
pub const ExtPub = struct {
    depth: u8,
    parent_fp: [fingerprint_bytes]u8,
    child_num: [4]u8, // big-endian
    chain_code: [key_bytes]u8,
    pubkey: [key_data_bytes]u8, // compressed serP(K), 33 bytes
};

/// Neuter an extended private key to its extended public key — same tree position, the private scalar replaced by serP(point(k)). This is what
/// `serialize_xpub` serializes; `neuter` exposes the node so a public-only party can carry and derive from it.
pub fn neuter(ext: *const ExtKey) Bip32Error!ExtPub {
    const pub_key = try serialize_public(&ext.key);
    return ExtPub{
        .depth = ext.depth,
        .parent_fp = ext.parent_fp,
        .child_num = ext.child_num,
        .chain_code = ext.chain_code,
        .pubkey = pub_key,
    };
}

/// The four-byte fingerprint of an extended PUBLIC key: the first four bytes of HASH160 of its compressed public key. The public sibling of
/// `fingerprint`, reading the pubkey it already holds rather than deriving one.
pub fn fingerprint_pub(ext: *const ExtPub) Bip32Error![fingerprint_bytes]u8 {
    const h = try bc.hash160(&ext.pubkey);
    var out: [fingerprint_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < fingerprint_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        assert(i < fingerprint_bytes);
        out[i] = h[i];
    }
    return out;
}

/// The field element 1 — the Z coordinate that lifts an affine point (x, y) into projective (x : y : 1) for the group law.
fn fe_one() fe.Field {
    var one_be = [_]u8{0} ** key_bytes;
    one_be[key_bytes - 1] = 1;
    return fe.from_bytes(&one_be);
}

/// Lift an affine public key (x, y) big-endian into the projective Point the group law adds, with Z = 1.
fn lift_affine(x_be: *const [key_bytes]u8, y_be: *const [key_bytes]u8) pt.Point {
    return pt.Point{ .x = fe.from_bytes(x_be), .y = fe.from_bytes(y_be), .z = fe_one() };
}

/// Derive a public child (CKDpub) — the public tree without the private half. For a NORMAL index i, K_child = point(parse256(I_L)) + K_par, where
/// I = HMAC-SHA-512(c_par, serP(K_par) ‖ ser32(i)); the child chain code is I_R. A hardened index is impossible from a public parent, and an
/// I_L ≥ n or an infinite child point is refused (BIP-32 §CKDpub). Composes only GREEN stones: the HMAC, the public-key derivation and parse,
/// the complete group-law addition, and the SEC1 compression — authoring no new cryptography.
pub fn ckd_pub(parent: *const ExtPub, index: u32) Bip32Error!ExtPub {
    // invariant: a public parent cannot derive a hardened child — hardening needs the private key it does not hold.
    if (index >= hardened_offset) return error.HardenedFromPublic;
    // invariant: depth never wraps.
    if (parent.depth == 255) return error.InvalidChild;

    var data: [ckd_data_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var j: u32 = 0;
    while (j < key_data_bytes) : (j += 1) {
        assert(j < key_data_bytes);
        data[j] = parent.pubkey[j];
    }
    const idx = ser32(index);
    j = 0;
    while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
        assert((key_data_bytes + j) < ckd_data_bytes);
        data[key_data_bytes + j] = idx[j];
    }

    const i = try hmac.mac(&data, &parent.chain_code);
    var il: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var ir: [key_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < key_bytes) : (k += 1) {
        assert(k < key_bytes and (key_bytes + k) < i_bytes);
        il[k] = i[k];
        ir[k] = i[key_bytes + k];
    }

    // invariant: I_L must be a canonical scalar (< n); BIP-32 refuses I_L ≥ n rather than reducing it.
    const il_le = reverse32(&il);
    if (!sc.is_canonical(&il_le)) return error.InvalidChild;

    // point(I_L) = I_L·G, obtained by deriving its public key and parsing back to affine coordinates (composing the GREEN rungs, no raw ladder).
    const il_pub = try pk.from_private_compressed(&il);
    const il_point_aff = try pk.parse(&il_pub);
    const parent_point_aff = try pk.parse(&parent.pubkey);

    // K_child = point(I_L) + K_par over the complete group law.
    const sum = pt.add(lift_affine(&il_point_aff.x, &il_point_aff.y), lift_affine(&parent_point_aff.x, &parent_point_aff.y));

    // invariant: the child point is not the identity — an infinite child key is refused, not serialized (its Z packs to all zero).
    const z_bytes = fe.to_bytes(sum.z);
    if (is_zero32(&z_bytes)) return error.InvalidChild;

    const cx = pt.affine_x(sum);
    const cy = pt.affine_y(sum);
    const child_pubkey = pk.compress_xy(&cx, &cy);

    const fp = try fingerprint_pub(parent);
    return ExtPub{
        .depth = parent.depth + 1,
        .parent_fp = fp,
        .child_num = idx,
        .chain_code = ir,
        .pubkey = child_pubkey,
    };
}

/// Serialize an extended PUBLIC key as its "xpub…" text — the same envelope `serialize_xpub` writes, over a node that never held a private key.
pub fn serialize_ext_pub(dest: []u8, ext: *const ExtPub) Bip32Error![]const u8 {
    // The ExtKey and ExtPub share every serialized field but the key body; reuse the frame layout by mapping the shared fields.
    const shim = ExtKey{
        .depth = ext.depth,
        .parent_fp = ext.parent_fp,
        .child_num = ext.child_num,
        .chain_code = ext.chain_code,
        .key = undefined, // unused — key_data below carries the public body
    };
    const frame = frame_bytes(&shim, &version_xpub, &ext.pubkey);
    return base58_frame(dest, &frame);
}

fn slice_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // BIP-32 Test Vector 1 — the spec's own published known-answer. The seed and every node's xprv/xpub are the spec's, quoted character for
    // character; nothing here is fabricated, and a one-bit error anywhere in the derivation or the envelope would break a match.
    const seed = [_]u8{ 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04, 0x05, 0x06, 0x07, 0x08, 0x09, 0x0a, 0x0b, 0x0c, 0x0d, 0x0e, 0x0f };

    // The six chain nodes, each with the spec's published xprv and xpub.
    const paths = [_]struct { xprv: []const u8, xpub: []const u8 }{
        .{ // m
            .xprv = "xprv9s21ZrQH143K3QTDL4LXw2F7HEK3wJUD2nW2nRk4stbPy6cq3jPPqjiChkVvvNKmPGJxWUtg6LnF5kejMRNNU3TGtRBeJgk33yuGBxrMPHi",
            .xpub = "xpub661MyMwAqRbcFtXgS5sYJABqqG9YLmC4Q1Rdap9gSE8NqtwybGhePY2gZ29ESFjqJoCu1Rupje8YtGqsefD265TMg7usUDFdp6W1EGMcet8",
        },
        .{ // m/0H
            .xprv = "xprv9uHRZZhk6KAJC1avXpDAp4MDc3sQKNxDiPvvkX8Br5ngLNv1TxvUxt4cV1rGL5hj6KCesnDYUhd7oWgT11eZG7XnxHrnYeSvkzY7d2bhkJ7",
            .xpub = "xpub68Gmy5EdvgibQVfPdqkBBCHxA5htiqg55crXYuXoQRKfDBFA1WEjWgP6LHhwBZeNK1VTsfTFUHCdrfp1bgwQ9xv5ski8PX9rL2dZXvgGDnw",
        },
        .{ // m/0H/1
            .xprv = "xprv9wTYmMFdV23N2TdNG573QoEsfRrWKQgWeibmLntzniatZvR9BmLnvSxqu53Kw1UmYPxLgboyZQaXwTCg8MSY3H2EU4pWcQDnRnrVA1xe8fs",
            .xpub = "xpub6ASuArnXKPbfEwhqN6e3mwBcDTgzisQN1wXN9BJcM47sSikHjJf3UFHKkNAWbWMiGj7Wf5uMash7SyYq527Hqck2AxYysAA7xmALppuCkwQ",
        },
        .{ // m/0H/1/2H
            .xprv = "xprv9z4pot5VBttmtdRTWfWQmoH1taj2axGVzFqSb8C9xaxKymcFzXBDptWmT7FwuEzG3ryjH4ktypQSAewRiNMjANTtpgP4mLTj34bhnZX7UiM",
            .xpub = "xpub6D4BDPcP2GT577Vvch3R8wDkScZWzQzMMUm3PWbmWvVJrZwQY4VUNgqFJPMM3No2dFDFGTsxxpG5uJh7n7epu4trkrX7x7DogT5Uv6fcLW5",
        },
        .{ // m/0H/1/2H/2
            .xprv = "xprvA2JDeKCSNNZky6uBCviVfJSKyQ1mDYahRjijr5idH2WwLsEd4Hsb2Tyh8RfQMuPh7f7RtyzTtdrbdqqsunu5Mm3wDvUAKRHSC34sJ7in334",
            .xpub = "xpub6FHa3pjLCk84BayeJxFW2SP4XRrFd1JYnxeLeU8EqN3vDfZmbqBqaGJAyiLjTAwm6ZLRQUMv1ZACTj37sR62cfN7fe5JnJ7dh8zL4fiyLHV",
        },
        .{ // m/0H/1/2H/2/1000000000
            .xprv = "xprvA41z7zogVVwxVSgdKUHDy1SKmdb533PjDz7J6N6mV6uS3ze1ai8FHa8kmHScGpWmj4WggLyQjgPie1rFSruoUihUZREPSL39UNdE3BBDu76",
            .xpub = "xpub6H1LXWLaKsWFhvm6RVpEL9P4KfRZSW7abD2ttkWP3SSQvnyA8FSVqNTEcYFgJS2UaFcxupHiYkro49S8yGasTvXEYBVPamhGW6cFJodrTHy",
        },
    };

    // The derivation path indices, in order: 0H, 1, 2H, 2, 1000000000. The first entry (m) has no index.
    const master = try master_from_seed(&seed);
    var node = master;

    var xprv_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
    var xpub_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
    var neuter_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
    var ckdpub_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;

    // Node 0 is the master; nodes 1..5 each derive from the previous by the matching index.
    const indices = [_]u32{ hardened_offset + 0, 1, hardened_offset + 2, 2, 1000000000 };

    var step: u32 = 0;
    while (step < paths.len) : (step += 1) {
        if (step > 0) {
            const index = indices[step - 1];
            const parent_node = node;
            node = try ckd_priv(&node, index);

            // CKDpub: for a NORMAL index, the neutered parent reaches the SAME child public key with no private key — the watch-only path. Its
            // serialized xpub must equal the spec's published xpub for this node, proving point(I_L) + K_par over the group law is correct.
            if (index < hardened_offset) {
                const parent_pub = try neuter(&parent_node);
                const child_pub = try ckd_pub(&parent_pub, index);
                const got_ckdpub = try serialize_ext_pub(&ckdpub_buf, &child_pub);
                if (!slice_eq(got_ckdpub, paths[step].xpub)) return error.CkdPubMismatch;
            }
        }

        const got_xprv = try serialize_xprv(&xprv_buf, &node);
        const got_xpub = try serialize_xpub(&xpub_buf, &node);
        if (!slice_eq(got_xprv, paths[step].xprv)) return error.XprvMismatch;
        if (!slice_eq(got_xpub, paths[step].xpub)) return error.XpubMismatch;

        // Neuter: the neutered node serialized as an ExtPub reproduces the very same xpub the private node's serialize_xpub does.
        const npub = try neuter(&node);
        const got_neuter = try serialize_ext_pub(&neuter_buf, &npub);
        if (!slice_eq(got_neuter, paths[step].xpub)) return error.NeuterMismatch;

        // invariant: the neutered public serialization agrees with the private node's own depth and fingerprint — the two forms are one node.
        assert(node.depth == @as(u8, @intCast(step)));
    }

    // A public parent cannot derive a hardened child — CKDpub refuses HardenedFromPublic before touching the HMAC.
    {
        const npub = try neuter(&master);
        if (ckd_pub(&npub, hardened_offset + 0)) |_| return error.HardenedSlipped else |err| if (err != error.HardenedFromPublic) return error.WrongHardenedErr;
    }

    // Edge refusals: a seed too short and a seed too long each raise BadSeedLength before the HMAC runs.
    {
        const short = [_]u8{0} ** 8;
        if (master_from_seed(&short)) |_| return error.SeedLengthSlipped else |err| if (err != error.BadSeedLength) return error.WrongSeedErr;

        const long = [_]u8{0} ** 65;
        if (master_from_seed(&long)) |_| return error.SeedLengthSlipped2 else |err| if (err != error.BadSeedLength) return error.WrongSeedErr2;
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-bip32: BIP-32 hierarchical-deterministic keys authored in pure Rye as a composition over the GREEN HMAC-SHA-512 " ++
        "(crypto/hmac_sha512.rye, the derivation PRF), secp256k1 public-key derivation and SEC1 compression (crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye), the " ++
        "scalar reduction modulo n (crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye, finishing (I_L + k_par) mod n), HASH160 (crypto/bitcoin_address.rye, the parent " ++
        "fingerprint), and Base58Check (encoding/base58check.rye, the xprv/xpub envelope) — authoring no new cryptography. master_from_seed runs " ++
        "the fixed \"Bitcoin seed\" HMAC, ckd_priv derives both hardened (0x00‖ser256(k_par)‖ser32(i)) and normal (serP(point(k_par))‖ser32(i)) " ++
        "children, neuter/ckd_pub derive the public tree (K_child = point(I_L) + K_par over the complete group law) without a private key, and " ++
        "serialize_xprv/serialize_xpub/serialize_ext_pub wrap any node. Parity holds the replayable way, against BIP-32's own published Test " ++
        "Vector 1: from the published seed 000102…0f, all six chain nodes (m, m/0H, m/0H/1, m/0H/1/2H, m/0H/1/2H/2, m/0H/1/2H/2/1000000000) " ++
        "serialize to EXACTLY the spec's published xprv AND xpub strings, character for character; each node's NEUTER reproduces the same xpub, " ++
        "and for every normal index CKDpub reaches that node's xpub from the neutered parent alone — a one-bit error in the HMAC, the modular add, " ++
        "the point addition, the compression, the fingerprint, or the envelope would break a match. A too-short and a too-long seed each refuse " ++
        "BadSeedLength, and a hardened index from a public parent refuses HardenedFromPublic. Purely local — every scalar is a TEST key from the " ++
        "spec's own seed, no network, no funds; signing with a real identity key stays the custody gate.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
